WEBVTT

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:05.730
Gauge rulers were invented to eliminate
the multiplication and division

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:09.480
and any errors that can occur.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:15.667
Use the ruler that's appropriate
for your marked stitch and row section.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:22.170
To determine the stitches,
use the side of the ruler marked S,

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:27.200
and place it between the markers,
and read from the ruler

00:00:27.266 --> 00:00:30.400
from right to left.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:33.480
Now, remember, we're measuring the columns

00:00:33.546 --> 00:00:38.280
of stitches between the markers.

00:00:38.346 --> 00:00:43.090
Measuring over 30 stitches,
my gauge ruler indicates that I have 14.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:49.200
5 to 15 stitches
per 4 inches or 10 centimeters.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:54.090
Measure the rows with the
gauge ruler the same way.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:59.120
Be sure to flip the ruler
over and use the R side.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:04.240
Again, be sure to measure the entire
stitch of both the starting and the ending

00:01:04.306 --> 00:01:07.730
row between those marker rows.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:12.200
Just picture if you have taken off those
marker rows, you want to do

00:01:12.266 --> 00:01:13.440
the loops on both edges.

00:01:13.506 --> 00:01:17.240
You want to get all of that whole section.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:23.890
The ruler confirms that measuring over 40
rows, we have 22 stitches

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:27.240
to 4 inches or 10 centimeters.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:30.610
These are the numbers we'll use to

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:36.970
calculate our pattern or to match gauge.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:41.890
These are the critical numbers
to making sure that what you knit

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:45.000
ends up being the right size.

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:50.840
Gauge rulers, regular rulers, either way,
do your best to measure

00:01:50.906 --> 00:01:52.600
correctly the first time.

